WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.900
GPT-6 Astra just started rolling out and can now take action on its own in creative tools like Blender. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 156. You know that feeling when you ask an ai for help, and it almost gets it but not quite? You wanted a plan for your science project, and it handed you a wall of facts instead. Open ai's newest model called GPT-6 Astra started reaching people today, and that missed

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:58.320
match is the thing they tried to fix. A model is just the brain behind chat GPT. Astra is the newest version of that brain. It is rolling out first to a limited group of organizations. Over the next few days, access expands to chat GPT-plus, pro, business, and enterprise users. Those are the paid chat GPT plans. It also reaches the open ai API, which is the doorway other apps used to plug into these models.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:23.480
And Amazon Web Services, or AWS. If you do not have it yet, that is completely normal. It is spreading in waves, not all at once. Open ai says this version is much better at understanding what people actually want. Think of your phone's auto-complete suddenly becoming so good it does not just finish your sentence. It figures out the bigger goal you are aiming for, and starts helping with that goal.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:45.400
They also say it is their most aligned model yet. Alignment here means staying closer to what you asked for instead of wandering off into unrelated suggestions. Picture a helpful classmate who keeps sliding extra topics onto your group project. Alignment is the difference between that classmate, and one who actually listens, then sticks to the assignment.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:02:12.140
Open ai also says Astra hits the best known results on some of the hardest tests researchers use right now. Those tests include Frontier Math Tier 4, ARC AGI 3, and Terminal Bench 4.0, plus strong scores on science and health challenges. You do not need to memorize those names. The simple version is this. On tough math and science puzzles, it is scoring at the top of the class.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:38.760
Here is why that matters in real life, not just on a scoreboard. Artificial intelligence or ai is starting to move from answering one question at a time to doing multi-step work on its own. For school, that could mean help breaking a messy science project into smaller steps, even if your wording is not perfect. For art and games, it could mean an assistant that keeps working on a 3d scene when your instructions are a little vague.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:54.640
You might notice it asking a clarifying question, or suggesting a useful next step instead of guessing wrong and charging ahead. Open ai recommends the free chat GPT desktop app to feel this version at its best, once it reaches your account.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:10.520
So here is something you can actually do. Download that free chat GPT desktop app from Open ai's site, then log in with chat GPT plus when the update shows up. If you are under 18, you may need a parents help to use a plus account.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:22.800
Start with a slightly open-ended request, like, help me design a simple game level, and notice whether it stays on your goal or drifts. Compare that with how older versions used to reply.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:33.860
The interesting part is not flashy language. It is whether the answer still feels like your idea. That intent reading skill is the quiet upgrade underneath all the headline scores.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:46.080
And once you have seen it miss or nail your meaning, the next question is obvious. How does a computer even learn what you meant? A close friend can read a sloppy text and still know what you wanted.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:04:11.880
They use your usual style, the topic you have been circling, and the fact that you probably want help, not a random lecture. Now stretch that idea. Imagine an ai doing a similar kind of guesswork, except it has practiced on millions of past conversations at once. It picks up patterns like this. When someone says they are stuck on a project, they usually want a plan, or a quick example, not a textbook chapter.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:28.000
Older models often treated every request like a brand new riddle with no context. Astra has been trained with extra focus on those human intent patterns, so it fills in the gaps instead of starting from zero every time. Walk through it like a group chat.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:39.660
First, you send a short, slightly unclear message. Second, a person who knows you well checks the recent thread, your tone, and what help usually means in that situation.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:05:04.200
Third, they reply with a plan or a sample, and maybe one question if something is still fuzzy. The model is doing a machine version of that third step. It is matching your words to patterns of what people in that situation typically need. That is why Oppen ai calls it their most aligned version. It has been tuned to stay nearer your real goal instead of sprinting down a clever side path.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:14.680
The result is supposed to feel less like texting a brilliant stranger, and more like working with someone who has been paying attention to how you like things done. It will still get things wrong.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:29.020
Alignment is a seatbelt, not a guarantee you never take a wrong turn. But the next time a reply feels oddly on target, you will know it is not magic. It is pattern matching, practiced at a huge scale, aimed at your intent.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:35.600
Once that clicks, the fun part is watching what happens when the model stops only talking, and starts doing.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:47.900
A researcher named Ethan Mollick gave GPT-6 Astra an open-ended request inside Blender, which is a popular program for building 3D worlds, game assets, and animations.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:06:00.260
The model did not just give advice. It spun up helper agents to research and check its own work, then began making changes. Helper agents are like extra teammates the main AI recruits.

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:09.480
One looks things up, another double checks, and the first one keeps building. That is a shift from, tell me how, to, I will start working on it.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:23.120
If you like 3D art, game maps, or simple animations, that is a big deal. When you have access, open the chat GPT desktop app and describe a loose idea, like build a small floating island with trees.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:34.020
Watch whether it only chats or actually starts shaping the scene. Notice if it pauses to check itself the way a careful art student would. Same person, different test.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:46.980
Mollick also asked the model to create original research papers. The work was technically solid and still felt boring. He said it lacked research taste, meaning the instinct to pick questions that actually matter.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:56.100
Add is a useful reminder for school. A powerful AI can write a clean outline and still choose a yawn of a topic. Try this as a challenge.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:04.460
Ask chat GPT to generate a short research outline on a subject you care about. Then set it next to the question you would have picked yourself.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:12.740
If your version is more interesting, that is not a failure of the tool. That is your judgement doing the part the model still cannot fake.

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:20.120
Think of it like a kitchen that can follow any recipe perfectly, but still needs a human to decide which dinner is worth cooking.

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:34.320
A few smaller notes are worth parking in your brain before you go try things. A creator named Linus Eakinstam argued that current AI tests need updating, so they measure what matters in real use, not just academic scores.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:45.200
That is like grading a basketball player only on free throws, then wondering why the real game feels different. How we judge progress will keep changing as these tools get more capable.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:56.600
A top score on a worksheet is not the same as being useful on your homework. Users are also sharing examples of Astra improving creative coding prompts in a niche tool called Twiggle.

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:10.560
In one case, it took a basic description and turned it into a richer, neo-gothic shader prompt. A shader is a tiny set of instructions for how light and texture should look, like the gloom on old stone or the shine on wet metal.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:18.620
It is a small, fun sign that these models can help with artistic experiments, even in apps most people have never heard of.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:30.460
If coding art is your thing, try feeding a plain visual idea into chat GPT and asking it to make the description more specific, then see whether the picture in your head got sharper.

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:35.960
None of this replaces your taste, it just gives you a louder first draft to argue with.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:48.080
That's it for today. Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now. If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it. That's literally how learning works.

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:51.680
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow.

